==Orthography==
===Consonants===
At the end of the Middle Irish period around 1200, scribes began using digraphs ''bh, gh, mh'' to indicate the fricatives /v/, /ɣ/, and /ṽ/ (lenited versions of /b/, /g/, and /m) by analogy with the lenited ''ch, th, ph''. Lenition of these respective stops went unmarked.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Ó Baoill |first1=Colm |editor1-last=Macleod |editor1-first=Michelle |editor2-last=Watson |editor2-first=Moray |title=The Edinburgh Companion to the Gaelic Language |date=2010 |publisher=Edinburgh University Press|page=6 |isbn=9780748637102 |url=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/Edinburgh_Companion_to_the_Gaelic_Langua/GwurBgAAQBAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=middle+irish+bh+digraph&pg=PA6&printsec=frontcover |access-date=30 April 2026 |chapter=A History of Gaelic to 1800}}</ref> Diacritics derived fround the [[Ancient Greek language|Ancient Greek]] [[rough breathing]] mark (◌̔) could also be used to represent lenition.{{efn-num|In this article, the early modern ''ponc séimhithe'', or [[Dot (diacritic)#Overdot|overdot]], is used to represent diacritical lenition representations.}} However, both methods remained sporadic and irregular until at least the 16th century, in the [[Early Modern Irish]] period.<ref name="Russell 1995">{{cite book |last1=Russell |first1=Paul |title=An Introduction to the Celtic Languages |date=1995 |publisher=Taylor & Francis |location=London, United Kingdom |pages=224 |isbn=9781317894551}}</ref>

